readsheets that  we had  in the stack so this is like a massive  amount of knowledge that any one person  would have to have the good news is that  there's no such thing as a full stack  data scientist so if that's giving you  anxiety great you don't have to worry  about all of those things and there's no  such thing as a full stack engineer and  anyone that tells you that they are a  full second genera is really minimizing  the contributions of all of the people  around them that are making the  technology work but we can build full  stack teams and so that's what the last  part of this talk is about and so that  was a long-winded introduction to what I  really want to talk about which is a  model for how to build a full stack data  science team so these are the the the  Mars rovers these are three generations  of Mars rovers there's the little cute  one which was named Oh what was its name  I forget Sojourner was that Sojourner  that was Sojourner and then there is  spirit I think or one of the Spirit  Opportunity Rovers and then curiosity  and so this is a test that NASA ran  testing these Rovers in the desert I  think in Arizona in the United States  and this is interesting because in order  to validate the technology NASA had to  take these Rovers and create an  environment that was similar to Mars  except where on earth were not on Mars  so it's very difficult to to find that  environment but there's no way that they  could launch a multi-billion dollar  device to another planet without having  tried to at least validate it in a  realistic environment and so in order to  measure the development of complex  systems in the 1970s NASA developed  something called the Technol technology  readiness level scale or TRL TRL's were  nine levels of technology readiness from  one which represented basic research to  nine which represented a system being  proven in an operational environment and  these models were really useful to  target where you were in the development 