is  process involves more than just your  engineering teams  the question is where do we fit data  science in this and right now most data  science teams within orgs are really at  the early levels we're doing algorithm  development we're doing research on our  data we're maybe building some  technology and then we do some stuff and  then hopefully we get it to running in  prod and then if we're really good about  closing the loop we also do analytics on  what we're doing on whether or not  that's been effective and then we feed  that back into the next development  cycle so that we continue iterating on  our product so let's kind of go through  and talk about data science readiness in  this framework so basic principles  observed that might be like algorithm  design and then we move up to technology  concept formulated that's kind of like  exploring our data doing feature  extraction maybe doing some basic  descriptive statistics the next step  experimental proof of concept that  doesn't need any adaptation at all this  is a lot of what we do in say a Jupiter  notebook or in whatever the our version  is I don't use our technology validated  in lab well maybe we validate our  algorithms against sample data once we  have the actual code working well maybe  you know hopefully we do cross  validation or some other type of  validation when we develop our  algorithms and then we look at the  relevant environment well that's prade  data demonstrating tech in the relevant  environment well that's when we start to  integrate it with the rest of our  technology and then the last three three  levels need no change this is exactly  where we want to be they very accurately  describe in my opinion what a mature  data science product looks like and we  can kind of conceptually separate this  into discovery versus delivery the early  phases our discovery the later phases  our delivery and these are two different  skill sets that you need as a data  scientist so let's go through th